Q: How does Qt expect me to link QML modules? A: <p>You were quite close to the solution.</p>
<p>After adding a call to <code>qt_standard_project_setup()</code> in CMake
and switching to using the versionless commands, your example works without issues.
I have also dropped the <code>NO_RESOURCE_TARGET_PATH</code> and loaded your QML
file in <code>main.cpp</code> a bit differently.</p>
<p>Here is the updated version:</p>
<pre class="lang-none prettyprint-override"><code># CMakeLists.txt

cmake_minimum_required(VERSION 3.22)

project(StackOverflowQmlModuleQuestion VERSION 0.1 LANGUAGES CXX)

find_package(Qt6 REQUIRED COMPONENTS Core Gui Qml)
qt_standard_project_setup(REQUIRES 6.8)

add_subdirectory(Ui)

qt_add_executable(Application main.cpp)
qt_add_qml_module(Application URI Application QML_FILES Main.qml)
target_link_libraries(Application PRIVATE Qt6::Gui Qt6::Qml Ui)
</code></pre>
<pre class="lang-none prettyprint-override"><code>#  Ui/CMakeLists.txt

find_package(Qt6 REQUIRED COMPONENTS Qml)

qt_add_qml_module(Ui URI Ui QML_FILES MyComponent.qml)
</code></pre>
<pre class="lang-cpp prettyprint-override"><code>// main.cpp

#include &lt;QtGui/QGuiApplication&gt;
#include &lt;QtQml/QQmlApplicationEngine&gt;

auto main(int argumentCount, char* arguments[]) -&gt; int
{
    QGuiApplication application(argumentCount, arguments);
    QQmlApplicationEngine engine;
    engine.loadFromModule(&quot;Application&quot;, &quot;Main&quot;);

    return application.exec();
}
</code></pre>
<pre class="lang-none prettyprint-override"><code>// Main.qml

import QtQuick
import Ui

Window {
    visible: true

    MyComponent {
        anchors.fill: parent
    }
}
</code></pre>
<pre class="lang-none prettyprint-override"><code>// Ui/MyComponent.qml

import QtQuick
import QtQuick.Controls

Item {
    Button {
        anchors.centerIn: parent
        text: &quot;Click&quot;
    }
}
</code></pre>
<p>This was tested on Windows with <code>Qt 6.10.3 MinGW 13.1.0 64-bit</code>.</p>
<p>Contrary to what most examples on the internet suggests it is not a must
to link your QML modules statically by linking their plugin library to your application.
You can also link them dynamically via their backing library (as you tried to do).</p>
<p>I have created a repository that demonstrates the different linking options
with examples and collects resources on this topic.<br>
Link: <a href="https://github.com/KGergo88/QtQmlModuleExamples" rel="nofollow noreferrer">https://github.com/KGergo88/QtQmlModuleExamples</a></p>

Q: Asynchronous request and insertion of received nodes A: <p>It seems to me that you want a function to create an HTML element with some synchronously determined attributes (like the <code>className</code> or <code>textContent</code>) and some asynchronously determined children (for example, the result of an HTTP request).</p>
<p>Here is a simplified working example that illustrates what I mean. It creates a <code>&lt;ul&gt;</code> element with synchronously determined <code>style</code> and adds two children, which are <code>&lt;li&gt;</code> elements determined by an HTTP request whose response contains an array of strings <code>[&quot;Peter&quot;, &quot;Paul&quot;]</code>.</p>
<p><div class="snippet" data-lang="js" data-hide="false" data-console="true" data-babel="false" data-babel-preset-react="false" data-babel-preset-ts="false">
<div class="snippet-code">
<pre class="snippet-code-js lang-js prettyprint-override"><code>function el(t, p = {}, c) {
  const elem = document.createElement(t);
  Object.assign(elem, p);
  c?.then(function(children) {
    for (const ch of children)
      elem.appendChild(ch);
  });
  return elem;
}
document.body.appendChild(el(
  "ul",
  {style: "background: yellow"},
  fetch("https://httpbin.org/anything?child=Peter&amp;child=Paul")
  .then(r =&gt; r.json())
  .then(j =&gt; j.args.child.map(ch =&gt; el(
    "li",
    {textContent: ch}
  )))
));</code></pre>
</div>
</div>
</p>
<p>Unlike in your code, <code>c</code> is not an array of arguments to the function <code>el</code> but a promise that resolves to an array. This is necessary because the length of the array is not known when the function is invoked, it becomes known only asynchronously, when the response has been received.</p>
<p>Is this what you want to achieve?</p>
